  "worked_solution": "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(7x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5x)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(9x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x),\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(7x)\u27e9 & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(5x)+\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(9x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(10\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0))+\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(18\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{18}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(7x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product.",
